{
  "_comment": "Antigravity (agy) permission baseline. Merged by setup.sh into ~/.gemini/antigravity-cli/settings.json via claude/scripts/agy-apply-permissions.py (that file also holds trustedWorkspaces and per-machine grants, so it is never symlinked). Rules are word-by-word prefixes. Precedence: deny > ask > allow. Security model: the terminal sandbox is the boundary (toolPermission=proceed-in-sandbox, enableTerminalSandbox=true); it blocks ~/.ssh and .env files by default and isolates the filesystem and network. No local tool carries an unsandboxed(...) rule; only gh read subcommands run unsandboxed (git fetch/pull prompt, because repository config can name programs for git to run). Reads outside the workspace are served inside the sandbox through allowNonWorkspaceAccess and read_file(~/...) rules, which the merge script expands to the machine's home; read_file denies cover credential files exactly, because the file tool matches paths, not shell text. Withdrawing a rule from the permissions object does not withdraw it from a machine that already installed it, because apply is additive; the retired array lists rules the baseline has revoked. apply deletes any exact match from the allow list and check fails until it is gone; ask and deny are never touched, because the same rule sitting there is the operator's own hardening and removing it would turn a forbidden operation into an approvable one. Only withdrawn grants are listed for the same reason: a deny the baseline drops is left in place as harmless residue. There are deliberately no command regexes: shell syntax cannot be classified airtight (see the reverted #411), so command denies are plain prefixes that act as speed bumps inside the sandbox, not as a boundary.",
